---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Methodology">
  <h1 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Methodology</h1>

  <section class="mt-6 space-y-3">
    <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">Scope</h2>
    <p>
      This register covers organisations — companies, charities, membership bodies, trade
      associations, think tanks and unincorporated associations — that carry out advocacy or
      lobbying activity in relation to UK higher education. Coverage is designed to expand into
      health, public affairs, security, media and politics as sourced records are added.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each organisation is tagged with one or more sectors describing what its own registered
      purpose or documented activity shows: a subject-matter domain (higher education, health,
      security, media, politics), a specific field of practice (legal affairs, government
      affairs), or the audience it primarily serves (community). These tags describe an
      organisation's own stated purpose — they are not a ranking or a claim about its importance
      or influence.
    </p>
    <p>
      This register does not cover individuals. It does not contain named-person profiles,
      officer or trustee listings, or any claim, activity or funding record about a named
      individual. Where a source document names an individual, only the organisational fact the
      document establishes is recorded.
    </p>
    <p>
      Coverage includes organisations campaigning on Israel/Palestine within UK higher education
      and public affairs. Advocacy on this subject spans organisations holding opposing positions,
      and the register's commitment to that subject is to cover organisations on more than one
      side of it to the same sourcing standard — not to document one side of a contested issue in
      isolation. Where coverage of a topic is added incrementally, this page and the entries
      themselves make clear that coverage is partial and still being built out.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-8 space-y-3">
    <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">Contested and politically sensitive topics</h2>
    <p>
      Some organisations in this register campaign on subjects where reasonable people, and the
      organisations themselves, disagree — immigration, foreign policy, industrial relations,
      Israel/Palestine, and similar. The register does not take a position on any such subject.
      An entry documents what a specific dated document shows an organisation did — filed a
      return, submitted evidence, responded to a consultation, received a grant — not whether that
      activity was justified, effective, or right.
    </p>
    <p>
      An organisation's registered charitable or corporate purpose is recorded as that purpose,
      not reinterpreted. A body whose stated and evidenced purpose is community security, for
      example, is not described as a lobbying organisation unless a specific sourced record shows
      it engaging in advocacy activity (a consultation response, committee evidence, and so on) —
      and in that case, only that specific activity is recorded, not a general characterisation of
      the organisation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-8 space-y-3">
    <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">Sourcing standard</h2>
    <p>
      Every activity, funding and affiliation record cites at least one source. Every source
      records both the original document URL and an independently archived copy, so that a
      record remains verifiable if the original is later moved, edited or removed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Records are marked <strong>documented</strong> when drawn directly from a primary or
      statutory document (a filing, a register entry, a consultation response, a grant record),
      and <strong>reported</strong> when drawn from secondary reporting of a document or event
      that could not itself be directly obtained at time of entry.
    </p>
    <p>
      Press reporting is an accepted source, cited as <strong>reported</strong> confidence and
      attributed to the specific outlet. Acceptance depends on the outlet, not the format: the
      register uses independently edited news publications with a public corrections record, and
      does not use state-controlled propaganda outlets or other publications with a documented
      pattern of fabrication, for the same reason a newsroom would decline to cite them. A press
      report is only used for the specific claim it actually makes about the specific organisation
      named in it — not extended to an organisation the report does not mention.
    </p>
    <p>
      Descriptions state what a document shows, in a single factual sentence. The register does
      not assign ratings, severity levels, or evaluative judgements of any kind.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-8 space-y-3">
    <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">What is excluded</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
      <li>Individuals, named officers, trustees, or any person-level data.</li>
      <li>Allegations, opinions, or unsourced claims of any kind.</li>
      <li>Ratings, rankings, severity scores, or other evaluative measures.</li>
      <li>Records without a resolvable, archived source — these are not rendered.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-8 space-y-3">
    <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">Corrections and right of reply</h2>
    <p>
      Any organisation covered by this register may request a correction to a record, or submit a
      reply for publication alongside the records it responds to. Requests can be made using the
      contact details on the <a href="/corrections">corrections page</a>. All corrections and
      removals are logged publicly with the date and the reason for the change.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-8 space-y-3">
    <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">Submissions</h2>
    <p>
      Public submissions are accepted through the <a href="/submit">submission form</a>. No
      submission is published automatically. Every submission enters a moderation queue and is
      checked against a real source before any record is added.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-8 space-y-3">
    <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-500">Complaints</h2>
    <p>
      Complaints about the accuracy or fairness of a record can be sent to the address listed on
      the <a href="/corrections">corrections page</a>. Each complaint is logged and, where it
      results in a change, recorded in the public corrections log.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
